---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';

const title       = 'Raven vs Magpie — Size, Appearance, and Key Differences';
const description = 'Ravens and magpies are both corvids but differ dramatically in size, plumage, tail shape, and behaviour. Common Raven vs Eurasian Magpie comparison with field marks.';
const path        = '/compare/raven-vs-magpie/';

const breadcrumbs = [
  { name: 'Home',    url: '/' },
  { name: 'Compare', url: '/compare/' },
  { name: 'Raven vs Magpie', url: path },
];

const faq = [
  { q: 'What is the difference between a raven and a magpie?', a: 'Ravens and magpies are both corvids but are easily told apart. Ravens are very large (56–69 cm, 700–1600 g), all-black, with a wedge-shaped tail and massive bill. Eurasian Magpies are much smaller (44–46 cm, 200–250 g), boldly patterned in black and white with a very long graduated tail accounting for half their total length. They are not easily confused in the field.' },
];
---

<BaseLayout title={title} description={description} path={path} ogType="article"
  datePublished="2026-11-07" dateModified="2026-11-07"
  breadcrumbs={breadcrumbs} faq={faq} pageType="Article">

<article class="id-page">
  <nav class="id-breadcrumb" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
    <ol>
      {breadcrumbs.map((b, i) => (
        <li>
          {i < breadcrumbs.length - 1
            ? <><a href={b.url}>{b.name}</a><span aria-hidden="true"> › </span></>
            : <span aria-current="page">{b.name}</span>}
        </li>
      ))}
    </ol>
  </nav>
  <header class="id-header">
    <p class="id-pillar">Compare · Corvids</p>
    <h1>Raven vs Magpie</h1>
    <p class="id-subtitle">Common Raven <em>Corvus corax</em> vs Eurasian Magpie <em>Pica pica</em></p>
  </header>

  <section class="id-snippet" aria-label="Quick answer">
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Ravens and magpies are both corvids but differ dramatically. The raven is very large and all-black; the magpie is much smaller, boldly pied (black and white), and has a distinctive very long tail. In the field, the two are <strong>not easily confused</strong> — size, pattern, and tail shape make them immediately separable.</p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>At a Glance</h2>
    <div class="id-compare-table-wrap">
      <table class="id-compare-table">
        <thead>
          <tr><th>Feature</th><th>Common Raven</th><th>Eurasian Magpie</th></tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td>Length</td><td>56–69 cm</td><td>44–46 cm (inc. tail)</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Weight</td><td>700–1,600 g</td><td>200–250 g</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Wingspan</td><td>115–150 cm</td><td>52–62 cm</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Plumage</td><td>All black (iridescent)</td><td>Black and white; iridescent green-blue on wings and tail</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Tail</td><td>Wedge-shaped</td><td>Very long, graduated — accounts for ~50% of total length</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Bill</td><td>Massive, curved</td><td>Moderate, straight</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Call</td><td>Deep "kronk-kronk"</td><td>Harsh rattling "chak-chak-chak"</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Flight</td><td>Powerful, soaring; slow wingbeats</td><td>Weak, flapping; rarely soars</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Habitat</td><td>Mountains, boreal forest, coast, moorland</td><td>Open country, woodland edge, suburban areas</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Size</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> The size difference is dramatic:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>A Common Raven weighs 700–1,600 g — 3–8 times the weight of a magpie (200–250 g)</li>
      <li>The raven's wingspan (115–150 cm) is roughly double the magpie's (52–62 cm)</li>
      <li>The magpie's apparent length (44–46 cm) is similar to some measurements of smaller ravens, but most of the magpie's length is tail — the body is much smaller</li>
    </ul>
    <p>In the field, the size difference is immediately obvious when both species are in view together.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Plumage</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong></p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Common Raven:</strong> Entirely black with strong oil-green and blue-purple iridescence; throat hackles prominent in adults</li>
      <li><strong>Eurasian Magpie:</strong> Bold black-and-white — white belly, white shoulder patches visible in flight; wings and tail show strong iridescent blue-green; unmistakable pattern</li>
    </ul>
    <p>There is no plumage confusion possible between these species. The magpie's bold pied pattern is unique among common European corvids.<sup><a href="#src-1">1</a></sup></p>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Tail Shape</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Tail shape is a defining feature of both species in flight:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Raven:</strong> Wedge-shaped or diamond-shaped tail — the central feathers extend beyond the outer feathers. Key identification feature separating ravens from crows.</li>
      <li><strong>Magpie:</strong> Very long, strongly graduated tail — the central feathers are much longer than the outer ones, tapering to a point. This tail accounts for roughly half the bird's total length and is often used in display.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-section">
    <h2>Shared Features as Corvids</h2>
    <p><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> Both species are highly intelligent corvids with shared cognitive abilities:</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Both show advanced problem-solving, food caching, and social learning</li>
      <li>Magpies are the only non-mammal species known to pass the mirror self-recognition test (Prior et al. 2008 — though this finding has been contested)</li>
      <li>Both species are omnivorous generalists; magpies have a broadly similar diet breadth to ravens but at a smaller scale</li>
      <li>Both show the "shiny object" association in folk tradition — but experimental evidence for either species specifically preferring shiny objects is weak (Shephard et al. 2014)</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section class="id-faq id-section" aria-labelledby="faq-h2">
    <h2 id="faq-h2">Frequently Asked Questions</h2>
    {faq.map(item => (
      <details class="id-faq-item">
        <summary class="id-faq-q">{item.q}</summary>
        <p class="id-faq-a">{item.a}</p>
      </details>
    ))}
  </section>

  <section class="id-sources id-section" aria-labelledby="src-h2">
    <h2 id="src-h2">Sources</h2>
    <ol class="id-src-list">
      <li id="src-1">Madge, S. &amp; Burn, H. (1994). <em>Crows and Jays</em>. Christopher Helm. — species accounts for both Common Raven and Eurasian Magpie.</li>
      <li id="src-2">Prior, H., Schwarz, A. &amp; Güntürkün, O. (2008). Mirror-induced behaviour in the magpie (<em>Pica pica</em>): evidence of self-recognition. <em>PLOS Biology</em>, 6(8), e202. DOI: 10.1371/journal.pbio.0060202</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="id-claim-note"><strong class="claim-science">[science]</strong> field-guide and peer-reviewed data</p>
  </section>

  <nav class="id-nav-bottom">
    <a href="/compare/" class="id-back">← Comparisons</a>
    <a href="/compare/raven-vs-crow/" class="id-next">Raven vs Crow →</a>
  </nav>
</article>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .id-page { max-width: 820px; margin: 0 auto; padding: 2rem 1.25rem 4rem; color: var(--iron-ink, #e8e4d8); font-family: 'Inter', sans-serif; line-height: 1.72; }
  .id-breadcrumb ol { display: flex; flex-wrap: wrap; gap: .25rem; list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 2rem; font-size: .8rem; color: var(--ink-soft, #8a8070); }
  .id-breadcrumb a { color: var(--gold-500, #d4ae5a); text-decoration: none; }
  .id-pillar { font-size: .72rem; letter-spacing: .12em; text-transform: uppercase; color: var(--gold-500, #d4ae5a); margin: 0 0 .4rem; }
  .id-page h1 { font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: clamp(2rem, 5vw, 3.25rem); font-weight: 700; color: #fff; margin: 0 0 .2rem; line-height: 1.1; }
  .id-subtitle { font-size: 1rem; color: var(--ink-soft, #c0b89a); margin: 0 0 2rem; }
  .id-snippet { background: rgba(212,174,90,.08); border-left: 3px solid var(--gold-500, #d4ae5a); border-radius: 0 6px 6px 0; padding: 1rem 1.25rem; margin-bottom: 2.5rem; font-size: 1.03rem; }
  .id-section { margin-bottom: 2.75rem; }
  .id-section h2 { font-family: 'Cormorant Garamond', serif; font-size: 1.6rem; font-weight: 600; color: #fff; margin: 0 0 1rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(212,174,90,.2); padding-bottom: .4rem; }
  .id-section p { margin: 0 0 1rem; font-size: .97rem; }
  .id-section ul { padding-left: 1.25rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .id-section li { margin-bottom: .4rem; font-size: .95rem; }
  .id-compare-table-wrap { overflow-x: auto; margin-bottom: 1rem; }
  .id-compare-table { width: 100%; border-collapse: collapse; font-size: .88rem; }
  .id-compare-table th { background: rgba(212,174,90,.12); color: var(--gold-500,#d4ae5a); font-weight: 600; padding: .6rem .8rem; text-align: left; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(212,174,90,.3); }
  .id-compare-table td { padding: .55rem .8rem; border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.06); vertical-align: top; }
  .id-compare-table tr:last-child td { border-bottom: none; }
  .id-compare-table td:first-child { color: var(--ink-soft,#c0b89a); font-size: .82rem; white-space: nowrap; }
  .claim-science { color: #7ab9e0; font-style: normal; font-size: .78rem; }
  a[href] { color: var(--gold-500, #d4ae5a); }
  .id-faq-item { border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.07); }
  .id-faq-q { cursor: pointer; padding: .9rem 0; font-size: .97rem; font-weight: 500; color: #fff; list-style: none; }
  .id-faq-q::before { content: '+ '; color: var(--gold-500, #d4ae5a); font-weight: 700; }
  details[open] .id-faq-q::before { content: '− '; }
  .id-faq-a { margin: 0 0 1rem; line-height: 1.7; font-size: .93rem; color: var(--ink-soft, #c8c0b0); }
  .id-src-list { padding-left: 1.25rem; margin: 0 0 1rem; }
  .id-src-list li { margin-bottom: .6rem; font-size: .82rem; color: var(--ink-soft, #8a8070); line-height: 1.6; }
  .id-claim-note { font-size: .78rem; color: var(--ink-soft, #605848); border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.06); padding-top: .75rem; }
  .id-nav-bottom { display: flex; justify-content: space-between; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid rgba(255,255,255,.07); }
  .id-nav-bottom a { font-size: .9rem; text-decoration: none; }
  .id-nav-bottom a:hover { text-decoration: underline; }
</style>
